Q1. What is the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)?
Why do we need it?

Interview Ready Answer

The Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) states that

"Clients should not be forced to depend on
interfaces they do not use."

In simple words,

A class should implement only those methods
that it actually requires.

Instead of creating one large interface,
split it into multiple small,
role-specific interfaces.

Why do we need ISP?

• Avoid unnecessary method implementation.
• Reduce Coupling.
• Improve Maintainability.
• Improve Readability.
• Increase Flexibility.
• Prevent Empty or Unsupported Methods.

Real-Life Example

Restaurant

You order only Tea.

The waiter should bring only Tea,

not Tea + Coffee + Biscuits.

Similarly,

A class should receive only the methods
it actually needs.

